## Single candidate and API

The only candidate source is `scripts/research_score_bank_snapshot.py`.

- `capture(bank, outputs)` returns an opaque owned snapshot. Its payload
  comprehension, key sorting, attribute accesses and evaluation order are
  identical to the payload argument in frozen `_bank_state_digest`.
- `capture_value(value)` uses the same capture path for focused synthetic tests.
- `same(before, after)` returns an exact builtin bool for two valid handles.
  It rejects other arguments with `TypeError` and does not read input objects.
- A read-only `mode` property reports `supported` or `fallback`; it exposes no
  retained content. The handle is a private implementation object, not an
  adversarial security boundary or persisted format.

Each capture calls original `canonical` exactly once, retaining its original
NumPy conversion, subclass hooks, traversal and exceptions. There is no bulk
array shortcut, additional canonicalizer, input cache or mutable global patch.
For supported captures, original canonical has produced owned builtin
containers and immutable leaves. Retain that private tree; do not construct a
general-purpose binary representation or expose it to assembly.

The fresh eligibility traversal supports only exact builtin dict/list, ASCII
str keys and values, None, bool, finite float and bounded int. Root containers
have depth 1; the maximum is 32 container levels. Every value and dictionary
key occurrence is one node, including the root: at most 131072 nodes. All string
occurrences, including keys and repeated aliases, contribute their character
counts to a 4194304-character limit. Integers have at most 256 magnitude bits
using `bit_length()`. Active-path identities detect cycles only; aliases are
visited again. A supported tree must be completely examined before capture
returns. An unsupported or over-limit tree proceeds directly to the original
encoder; it cannot enter the content-comparison path.

Unsupported captures call original `encoded(snapshot)` immediately and retain
only its bytes. They never call `strict` or canonical again. Canonical or encoder
errors propagate at that capture, including before assembly could execute.
Non-ASCII input deliberately falls back: raw Unicode equality is not universally
equivalent to ensure-ASCII JSON (an astral character and a literal surrogate pair
can share the same JSON spelling).

For two supported captures, comparison preserves exact leaf types, sequence
order, dictionary key membership and values, while ignoring dictionary insertion
order. Finite float equality also distinguishes positive and negative zero.
Ordinary Python numeric equality alone is insufficient. Both complete captures
precede comparison, so a mismatch cannot skip validation or errors in the second
capture. Comparison may short-circuit only across already-owned validated trees.

For two fallback captures, compare their stored original bytes. For a mixed
pair, encode the owned supported tree with original `encoded` and compare against
the stored fallback bytes. Never reread the original bank or output object, and
never retry or suppress an unexpected error. Snapshot internals are not a public
mutation interface. No equality result changes any persisted hash or bytes.

